  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Bacteria are tiny, single-celled microorganisms found almost everywhere
    on Earth. They live in soil, water, air, food, and even inside the bodies of
    plants and animals. They are so small that they usually cannot be seen without
    a microscope. Bacteria come in different shapes, including spherical, rod-shaped,
    and spiral forms.

    A bacterial cell has a relatively simple structure. Unlike
    plant and animal cells, bacteria do not have a true nucleus. Their genetic material
    is found freely inside the cell. Most bacteria have a cell membrane and cell
    wall, which protect the cell and help maintain its shape. Some bacteria also
    have structures called flagella, which help them move.

    Bacteria can reproduce
    rapidly, usually by a process called binary fission, in which one bacterial cell
    divides into two cells. Under suitable conditions, their population can increase
    very quickly.

    Not all bacteria are harmful. Many bacteria are actually helpful
    and important. Some bacteria living in our digestive system help with digestion.
    Others are used to produce foods such as yogurt and cheese. Bacteria also act
    as decomposers, breaking down dead plants and animals and returning nutrients
    to the environment. Certain bacteria also help plants obtain essential nutrients.

    However,
    some bacteria can be harmful and cause diseases in humans, animals, and plants.
    Good hygiene, safe food handling, and proper medical treatment can help prevent
    many bacterial infections.

    Although bacteria are microscopic, they play
    a major role in human health, food production, and ecosystems.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
